<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khudayar Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1829">1829</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khudayar Khan was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>, holding the throne three times between 1845 and 1875 amid civil wars between settled and nomadic factions. Having lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and much territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he became its dependant, and his heavy taxes provoked a revolt in 1875 that drove him into exile. Russian troops then crushed the rebels and annexed the khanate in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khudayar-khan.jpg" alt="Khudayar Khan" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Commissioned by Konstantin Kaufman, compiled by Alexander Kun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sayid_Muhammad_Khudayar_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sayid Muhammad Khudayar Khan was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, the son of Shir Ali Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. When his father was killed in an uprising in 1845, Khudayar was in Namangan and survived. Murad Beg Khan was proclaimed khan for a short time, but the supporters of Shir Ali overthrew and later executed him as a puppet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. The military commander Mingbashi Musulmonqul then declared his son-in-law Khudayar khan, and through the 1840s the young ruler was kept in the palace while the Kipchak nomad elite held power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1853 an uprising against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> killed many of them, Musulmonqul among them, and Khudayar ruled in his own right on an anti-Kipchak, pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> course. When the northern provinces rebelled in 1858, his brother Malla Bek, sent to put them down, joined the rebels, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> with the help of the Kipchak warlord Alimqul and made himself khan. After Malla Bek was assassinated in 1862, Khudayar, allied with Kanaat, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, and helped by an advance of Emir Muzaffar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, returned to Kokand, but in 1863 Alimqul set up Muhammad Sultan Khan as khan and took Kokand and Tashkent.</p><p>Alimqul was killed opposing the Russians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> in 1865, and Khudayar entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> without resistance. In 1868 he formally accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. His rule was marked by very high taxes and a failing legal system, and a plot of 1870 to replace him with Seyid Khan, son of Malla Khan, ended in many executions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Driven out by the uprising of 1875, Khudayar was sent by the Russians to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orenburg">Orenburg</a>. Two years later he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>; with money from sympathetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> merchants he travelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and wandered through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> before settling, in the early 1880s, in the village of Karukh, east of Herat. He died there in poverty in 1886 and is buried there. On his orders his officer Niaz-Muhammad ibn Ashur-Muhammad Khoqani wrote the Tarikh-i Shahrukhi, a Persian chronicle of the khanate, and his palace remains a landmark of Kokand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Khudayar Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a revolt against the pro-Russian <b>Khudayar Khan</b> in 1875, General Skobelev crushed resistance and Russia annexed the khanate in 1876 as the Ferghana Oblast of the Turkestan Governorate-General.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-kokand">Khanate of Kokand</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Talas, in what is now <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.52,72.23&amp;year=1866" aria-label="Talas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Khudayar Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5765 3897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5765 3897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.52,72.23&amp;year=1866">What was here in 1866? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
